1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,462
Why isn't this a Spring bean?

2
00:00:01,542 --> 00:00:02,340
The class compiles,

3
00:00:02,340 --> 00:00:03,760
but the context has no

4
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,240
registration for it.

5
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:07,172
Asking for Client fails.

6
00:00:07,252 --> 00:00:08,260
Register FactoryConfig,

7
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:09,840
then refresh.

8
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,600
Its @Bean method returns the Client

9
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,296
that Spring manages.

10
00:00:13,376 --> 00:00:14,540
Or add the @Component annotation

11
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:15,780
to Worker,

12
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:18,420
then configure a scan of its package.

13
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:20,587
Spring discovers and registers it.

14
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:23,020
The scan covers lesson.scan.

15
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:24,700
Outside lives in another package,

16
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:26,983
so its annotation isn't picked up.

17
00:00:27,063 --> 00:00:28,140
Our three tests pass:

18
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:30,340
both registered beans are found;

19
00:00:30,340 --> 00:00:31,640
the class outside the scan

20
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:33,060
stays missing.

21
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:34,360
Registration tells Spring

22
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:35,890
what to manage.

23
00:00:37,890 --> 00:00:38,981
Thanks for watching.

